WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.667
-Our next guest
is a talented author

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:04.567
whose new novel "Assembly"
was published today.

00:00:04.567 --> 00:00:06.567
Please welcome to the show
Natasha Brown.

00:00:06.567 --> 00:00:08.901
Hello, Natasha. How are you?

00:00:08.901 --> 00:00:11.434
-Hi, Seth. I'm really good,
thank you. How are you?

00:00:11.434 --> 00:00:13.167
-I'm great.
So, this is very exciting,

00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:15.534
and I'm wondering
if you have nerves,

00:00:15.534 --> 00:00:18.067
because this book came out
a few months ago in the U.K.

00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:19.501
It was obviously
very well-received.

00:00:19.501 --> 00:00:21.501
Today, it's available
in the States.

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:23.567
Are you over the butterflies,
or are you excited

00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:27.801
that a whole new group of people
gets to be introduced to it?

00:00:27.801 --> 00:00:30.901
-I'm so excited to share it
with more people, I think.

00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:34.567
It's definitely been
a bit terrifying

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:36.434
the last couple of months,
but it's so nice

00:00:36.434 --> 00:00:40.734
to get a chance to bond
to readers over the book.

00:00:40.734 --> 00:00:42.234
-It's also your first novel.

00:00:42.234 --> 00:00:44.934
And, you know, that's an
impressive accomplishment enough

00:00:44.934 --> 00:00:47.033
to have your first novel
be so well-received,

00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:50.300
but was this always the novel
you were setting out to write

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:53.601
as your first endeavor?

00:00:53.601 --> 00:00:55.434
-Not really, actually.
I think I went into it

00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:58.234
not really knowing
what it was going to be.

00:00:58.234 --> 00:01:02.133
And all I knew was
I wanted to explore

00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:04.534
some of the ideas I'd been
reading about in nonfiction

00:01:04.534 --> 00:01:05.734
to try and find a story

00:01:05.734 --> 00:01:08.400
that would hopefully
bring them to life.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.767
And I just sort of
immersed myself

00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:12.801
in the world and the characters.

00:01:12.801 --> 00:01:14.767
And when I saw
where it was going,

00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:16.634
I just tried to follow it.

00:01:16.634 --> 00:01:18.701
-And you had
a background in finance.

00:01:18.701 --> 00:01:20.100
You did it for years.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.534
The main character in this book

00:01:21.534 --> 00:01:23.901
is a black British woman
in finance.

00:01:23.901 --> 00:01:26.634
Yet it's -- I mean, how much
of it is autobiographical,

00:01:26.634 --> 00:01:29.434
and how much of it is
just using your experiences

00:01:29.434 --> 00:01:32.267
to create
a completely new person?

00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:34.033
-Yeah, well, I think
we don't even really know much

00:01:34.033 --> 00:01:35.934
about her as a person,
and that was something

00:01:35.934 --> 00:01:37.801
I really consciously
wanted to do.

00:01:37.801 --> 00:01:40.267
I didn't want this novel
to look at her.

00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:43.267
I wanted to try and bring
the reader into her perspective

00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:45.334
and look out at the world
that she was in.

00:01:45.334 --> 00:01:48.534
So it definitely drew on kind of
people I've met,

00:01:48.534 --> 00:01:51.000
things I've seen.
But I really tried to make sure

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.033
it was almost
an immersive experience

00:01:53.033 --> 00:01:55.267
and tried to keep the details
about the narrator

00:01:55.267 --> 00:01:56.601
out of the story.

00:01:56.601 --> 00:01:58.601
-I did read
that there was a detail,

00:01:58.601 --> 00:02:02.601
maybe I guess
to sort of show excess,

00:02:02.601 --> 00:02:05.300
like, a very fancy high chair

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:06.901
that one of your friends
recognized

00:02:06.901 --> 00:02:10.167
was in fact the fancy high chair
that she had.

00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:12.234
-Yes. [ Laughs ]

00:02:12.234 --> 00:02:14.734
I saw her high chair,

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:18.367
and a few months later,
when she read the manuscript,

00:02:18.367 --> 00:02:22.601
I got a message saying,
"Is that my high chair?"

00:02:22.601 --> 00:02:26.868
But I think she's happy
that it's in a book now.

00:02:26.868 --> 00:02:28.734
-Okay, right.
It was worth every penny

00:02:28.734 --> 00:02:30.701
if it can show up in a book.

00:02:30.701 --> 00:02:34.067
So, this book talks about things
such as, you know, class

00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:37.234
and racism and sexism,
immigration.

00:02:37.234 --> 00:02:39.534
And yet at the core,
this narrator, I think,

00:02:39.534 --> 00:02:42.033
feels disassociated
maybe in a way

00:02:42.033 --> 00:02:45.801
that a lot of people from a lot
of different backgrounds feel.

00:02:45.801 --> 00:02:48.601
Like, so, you have this
very specific point of view,

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:50.868
you know, based on
the narrator's identity.

00:02:50.868 --> 00:02:53.000
But were you also aware
that you were writing, like,

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:58.234
I think, for me, ideas that are
really universal to all of us?

00:02:58.234 --> 00:03:01.567
-I think absolutely. And
I think, you know, as a reader,

00:03:01.567 --> 00:03:03.434
I've read sort of
the old English classics,

00:03:03.434 --> 00:03:05.234
and I get totally
absorbed in them

00:03:05.234 --> 00:03:06.968
and totally
feel for the characters

00:03:06.968 --> 00:03:08.667
and understand
where they're coming from,

00:03:08.667 --> 00:03:10.467
even if I've never been
in a horse and carriage

00:03:10.467 --> 00:03:13.300
or anything like that.
And I felt that by

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.267
just really leaning into the
specifics of this woman's life

00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:19.000
and trying to make it
as immersive

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.501
and believable as possible,

00:03:20.501 --> 00:03:25.100
it could sort of help show the
universality in her experience.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:28.067
-This is a super --

00:03:28.067 --> 00:03:29.634
I read it so quickly.

00:03:29.634 --> 00:03:31.868
It's not a long novel.
Have people been coming to you

00:03:31.868 --> 00:03:34.000
saying, like,
"You left me wanting more,"

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.067
and is that, as a writer,
something you're happy to hear?

00:03:37.067 --> 00:03:38.534
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:38.534 --> 00:03:41.734
I think there's definitely been
the "I wanted more" response

00:03:41.734 --> 00:03:45.534
and then also the slightly
sort of sheepish response

00:03:45.534 --> 00:03:47.634
that it's kind of nice
to have a short book.

00:03:47.634 --> 00:03:50.901
I'm definitely a fan
of short books, too, sometimes.

00:03:50.901 --> 00:03:52.801
-It is -- You feel
embarrassed to say it,

00:03:52.801 --> 00:03:54.567
but I am definitely
a fan of short books,

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:56.133
and thank you for writing one.

00:03:56.133 --> 00:03:58.000
[ Both laugh ]

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.434
I also wanted to ask where --

00:04:00.434 --> 00:04:02.367
Because I think this is the
thing that's most fascinating

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:05.300
to people, when they meet
someone who's written a novel,

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:09.501
when do you find yourself
most productive?

00:04:09.501 --> 00:04:12.000
-So, I'm one of those
really annoying morning people.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.367
I get up at like 5:00 or 6:00
in the morning,

00:04:14.367 --> 00:04:16.033
go down to the kitchen,
make coffee,

00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:19.033
and hopefully to get one
or two really productive hours,

00:04:19.033 --> 00:04:20.734
and it just tapers off
from then.

00:04:20.734 --> 00:04:22.801
-Well, that's a good way
of going about things.

00:04:22.801 --> 00:04:25.634
And I wanted -- Now I just
want to confirm a rumor.

00:04:25.634 --> 00:04:27.400
And I'm really hoping it's true.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.801
-[ Laughs ]
-I have heard --

00:04:29.801 --> 00:04:32.868
First of all, anytime
anyone across the pond

00:04:32.868 --> 00:04:34.968
watches our show,
I'm very happy.

00:04:34.968 --> 00:04:36.033
I'm very flattered.

00:04:36.033 --> 00:04:39.100
Is it true that you watch
"Corrections"?

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:40.901
-I do. I opted into
"Corrections."

00:04:40.901 --> 00:04:43.334
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.334 --> 00:04:46.067
-Thank you very much.

00:04:46.067 --> 00:04:48.133
Well, thank you so much.
We're always --

00:04:48.133 --> 00:04:51.701
You're far nicer than
what I expected from a jackal.

00:04:51.701 --> 00:04:55.234
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.234 --> 00:04:57.667
Hey, congratulations so much
on the book.

00:04:57.667 --> 00:04:59.467
It really is
such a wonderful read,

00:04:59.467 --> 00:05:02.367
and it's lovely meeting you.

00:05:02.367 --> 00:05:04.067
-Thank you so much.
Lovely to meet you.

00:05:04.067 --> 00:05:06.267
-"Assembly" is available now
wherever books are sold,

00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:07.400
but please remember

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:08.801
to support local
and independent bookstores.

